---
import Base from '../../../layouts/Base.astro';
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL;
---

<Base title="Performance - FastQC">
  <p class="label">Benchmarks</p>
  <h1>Performance</h1>
  <p style="color: var(--ink-muted); margin-bottom: 1rem">
    One file (7.3 GB Illumina WES), Apple M1 Pro, best of two runs. Java FastQC v0.13.0 is about
    3x faster than v0.12.1 at its default four threads, thanks to parallel processing
    <a href={`${base}about/upstream/`}>contributed upstream</a> from this project.
    Speedups are relative to v0.12.1.
  </p>

  <div class="grid-2" style="margin-bottom: 1.5rem">
    <div>
      <h3 class="bench-title">Default threads</h3>
      <div class="bench-chart">
        <div class="bench-row">
          <span class="bench-label bench-label-version">Java 0.12</span>
          <div class="bench-bar bench-bar-java bench-bar-old" style="width: 100%"></div>
          <span class="bench-time">7m 36s</span>
        </div>
        <div class="bench-row">
          <span class="bench-label bench-label-version">Java 0.13</span>
          <div class="bench-bar bench-bar-java" style="width: 36.6%"></div>
          <span class="bench-time">2m 47s</span>
          <span class="bench-speedup">2.7x</span>
        </div>
        <div class="bench-row">
          <span class="bench-label bench-label-version">Rust</span>
          <div class="bench-bar bench-bar-rust" style="width: 7.8%"></div>
          <span class="bench-time">36 s</span>
          <span class="bench-speedup">12.8x</span>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div>
      <h3 class="bench-title">Single thread (<code>-t 1</code>)</h3>
      <div class="bench-chart">
        <div class="bench-row">
          <span class="bench-label bench-label-version">Java 0.12</span>
          <div class="bench-bar bench-bar-java bench-bar-old" style="width: 87.4%"></div>
          <span class="bench-time">7m 36s</span>
        </div>
        <div class="bench-row">
          <span class="bench-label bench-label-version">Java 0.13</span>
          <div class="bench-bar bench-bar-java" style="width: 100%"></div>
          <span class="bench-time">8m 42s</span>
          <span class="bench-speedup">0.87x</span>
        </div>
        <div class="bench-row">
          <span class="bench-label bench-label-version">Rust</span>
          <div class="bench-bar bench-bar-rust" style="width: 13.1%"></div>
          <span class="bench-time">1m 9s</span>
          <span class="bench-speedup">6.7x</span>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="grid-2" style="margin-bottom: 1rem">
    <div>
      <h3 class="bench-title">Peak memory (RSS), default threads</h3>
      <div class="bench-chart">
        <div class="bench-row">
          <span class="bench-label bench-label-version">Java 0.12</span>
          <div class="bench-bar bench-bar-java bench-bar-old" style="width: 86.6%"></div>
          <span class="bench-time">426 MB</span>
        </div>
        <div class="bench-row">
          <span class="bench-label bench-label-version">Java 0.13</span>
          <div class="bench-bar bench-bar-java" style="width: 100%"></div>
          <span class="bench-time">492 MB</span>
        </div>
        <div class="bench-row">
          <span class="bench-label bench-label-version">Rust</span>
          <div class="bench-bar bench-bar-rust" style="width: 9.6%"></div>
          <span class="bench-time">47 MB</span>
          <span class="bench-speedup">9x less</span>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
  <p style="color: var(--ink-muted); font-size: 0.875rem; margin-bottom: 2.5rem">
    Defaults are one thread per file for v0.12.1, <code>-t 4</code> for v0.13.0 and up to
    6 threads for Rust. Java ran on OpenJDK 21. Measured October 2026.
  </p>

  <div style="margin-bottom: 2.5rem">
    <a href={`${base}about/performance/report/`} class="btn btn-primary">
      Full benchmark report
    </a>
  </div>

  <section style="margin-bottom: 3rem">
    <h2>Where the speed comes from</h2>
    <p style="color: var(--ink-muted); margin-bottom: 1rem">
      Three specific optimisations, each identified through CPU profiling:
    </p>
    <div class="grid-3">
      <div>
        <h3 style="font-size: 1rem; margin-top: 0">SIMD adapter search</h3>
        <p style="font-size: 0.875rem; color: var(--ink-muted)">
          Every adapter is found in a single SIMD pass over each read
          (<code>aho-corasick</code>'s Teddy), instead of one search per adapter.
          That search is 3.6–5.2x faster.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <h3 style="font-size: 1rem; margin-top: 0">Vectorised counting</h3>
        <p style="font-size: 0.875rem; color: var(--ink-muted)">
          Base counts and per-read quality minimums run as SIMD loops, and a 256-byte
          lookup table replaces branch chains in the per-base modules.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <h3 style="font-size: 1rem; margin-top: 0">Zero per-read allocation</h3>
        <p style="font-size: 0.875rem; color: var(--ink-muted)">
          FASTQ lines are parsed straight into reused record buffers with a SIMD
          newline search. No garbage collection overhead.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section style="margin-bottom: 3rem">
    <h2>Multi-threading</h2>
    <p style="color: var(--ink-muted); margin-bottom: 1rem">
      When a file gets two or more threads, it runs through a pipeline: a reader thread parses
      records and hands batches to analysis workers, each of which owns a subset of the QC
      modules. Every module still sees every read in order on one thread, so output is the same
      at any thread count. Without <code>-t</code>, FastQC-Rust uses the available CPUs, up to 6.
    </p>
    <div style="margin-bottom: 1rem; max-width: 36rem">
      <h3 class="bench-title">Wall clock time by <code>-t</code>, one 7.3 GB WES file (M1 Pro)</h3>
      <div class="bench-chart">
        <div class="bench-row">
          <span class="bench-label bench-label-wide">-t 1</span>
          <div class="bench-bar bench-bar-rust" style="width: 100%"></div>
          <span class="bench-time">68.2 s</span>
        </div>
        <div class="bench-row">
          <span class="bench-label bench-label-wide">-t 2</span>
          <div class="bench-bar bench-bar-rust" style="width: 78%"></div>
          <span class="bench-time">53.2 s</span>
          <span class="bench-speedup">1.28x</span>
        </div>
        <div class="bench-row">
          <span class="bench-label bench-label-wide">-t 3</span>
          <div class="bench-bar bench-bar-rust" style="width: 53%"></div>
          <span class="bench-time">36.2 s</span>
          <span class="bench-speedup">1.88x</span>
        </div>
        <div class="bench-row">
          <span class="bench-label bench-label-wide">-t 4</span>
          <div class="bench-bar bench-bar-rust" style="width: 53%"></div>
          <span class="bench-time">35.9 s</span>
          <span class="bench-speedup">1.90x</span>
        </div>
        <div class="bench-row">
          <span class="bench-label bench-label-wide">-t 6</span>
          <div class="bench-bar bench-bar-rust" style="width: 53%"></div>
          <span class="bench-time">35.9 s</span>
          <span class="bench-speedup">1.90x</span>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
    <p style="color: var(--ink-muted); margin-bottom: 1rem">
      A single file stops getting faster at around <code>-t 3</code>, where the analysis
      outpaces the file's one gzip decoder. Past that point, extra cores are better spent on
      more files at once.
    </p>
    <p style="color: var(--ink-muted)">
      For <code>.fastq.gz</code> input, decompression runs on its own thread via
      <a href="https://crates.io/crates/rapidgzip-core">rapidgzip-core</a> with a
      <a href="https://crates.io/crates/zlib-rs">zlib-rs</a> inflate, so it overlaps with the
      analysis. rapidgzip can split a file across decoders (<code>--decompress-threads</code>),
      but a single-member <code>.gz</code> can only be split speculatively, which costs several
      times the CPU and hundreds of MB for little gain, so one decoder per file is the default.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section style="margin-bottom: 3rem">
    <h2>Test dataset</h2>
    <p style="color: var(--ink-muted)">
      119 GB across 11 files: Illumina WES, Element AVITI, ONT PromethION long-read, and BAM.
      Apple Silicon (aarch64-apple-darwin).
      The <a href={`${base}about/performance/report/`}>full report</a> (April 2026, single-threaded,
      before Java v0.13.0 and Rust's parallel analysis) has per-file results,
      memory profiles, a CPU breakdown, and analysis of whether these optimisations could be back-ported to Java.
    </p>
  </section>
</Base>

<style>
.bench-title {
  font-family: var(--font-body);
  font-size: 0.8125rem;
  font-weight: 600;
  text-transform: uppercase;
  letter-spacing: 0.06em;
  color: var(--ink-muted);
  margin-top: 0;
  margin-bottom: 0.625rem;
}
.bench-row {
  display: flex;
  align-items: center;
  gap: 0.75rem;
  margin-bottom: 0.5rem;
}
.bench-label {
  font-size: 0.8125rem;
  font-weight: 600;
  width: 2.5rem;
  flex-shrink: 0;
  color: var(--ink-muted);
}
.bench-title code {
  text-transform: none;
}
.bench-label-version {
  width: 4.5rem;
}
.bench-bar-old {
  opacity: 0.55;
}
.bench-label-wide {
  width: 2.75rem;
  font-family: var(--font-mono);
}
.bench-bar {
  height: 1.5rem;
  border-radius: 3px;
  min-width: 4px;
}
.bench-bar-java {
  background: var(--color-java);
}
.bench-bar-rust {
  background: var(--brand-maroon);
}
.bench-time {
  font-family: var(--font-mono);
  font-size: 0.8125rem;
  color: var(--ink-muted);
  white-space: nowrap;
  flex-shrink: 0;
}
.bench-speedup {
  font-family: var(--font-heading);
  font-size: 0.875rem;
  font-weight: 700;
  color: var(--brand-maroon);
  white-space: nowrap;
  flex-shrink: 0;
}
</style>
